A||N||14||Sage||RICH START UP||||5||
Z||000000||Sage||05-28-2000||11:28 PM||||Anybody have any ideas what to look for first in an engine(2.8)that starts ok, but sounds as tho it is running on 4 or 5 cylinders for a minute or so.  Then it straightens up and runs and sounds fine.  When first started it puts out white smoke like unburned fuel, then when it smooths out, no more smoke.  It runs fine after initial warm up and gives no codes.  No ses light and doesn't seem to use an excessive amount of fuel.  I'm thinking cold start injector or leaking injector or fuel rail.  Anybody deal with this situation before, or have a clue as to where to look first?  TIA for any input.<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 05-29-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FieroGTRacingInc||05-29-2000||12:49 PM||||sage....  YES i have had that same situation with my 2.8L .... and ...  I still deal with it everyday...  after about 2-4 minutes it all clears out and run's real smooth... LOL<BR>I didnt have a clue exactly how to describe it to the forum, glad that you posted. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sage||05-29-2000||09:46 PM||||1frgt;This is in a car that I brought back from a fire.  It did this before the fire, but now that it is running again, I wanted to get everything up to par.  It is not one of my drivers, so don't know when I will tackle it full scale, just wanted to see if anyone had any experience, suggestions that might make it less time consuming.  If I come up with the solution, I will post it, maybe it will help you.  If you figure it out, how about doing the same?  Thought about fuel preasure regulator, but it runs too good after it smoothes out.  Have rebuilt the dukes, but have not as of yet tore into the 2.8 too far.  Don't even know for sure if it HAS a cold start injector!  The Haynes manual makes referance to it, but that doesn't mean it's there! LOL  If you come up with anything, LMK, and I will do the same.  Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||terryk||05-29-2000||10:41 PM||||Does this happen on a cold restart or a warm restart?<P>White smoke is coolant or oil. Unburned fuel is black. You could have bad seals and it is flooding a cylinder with oil or fouling a plug. You could have a blown head gasket, but the problem usually doesn't go away. Could be oil control rings, but again it usually doesn't go away. <P>How do the plugs look? If they are oily, then that leads to the problem. Does it puff smoke when it shifts?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||05-29-2000||11:55 PM||||sage,  terry is probably onto something...  The velve stem seals on the GM V6 engines are notorious for leaking and blowing a whole bunch of smoke at startup.  I would also check the plugs, if one or more are fouled due to oil you may need some work done.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sage||05-30-2000||01:02 AM||||Just changed the plugs and none of the old ones looked oil fouled.  It only does it on a cold start.  After it has been started and ran, it seems to be fine until it has had time to completly cool(several hours).  If you start it again during the day, it doesn't go through the routine, seems normal. Don't notice any smoke while driving.  It is an automatic tho, so might be hard to see any between shifts.  There is also smell of gas when you first start it.  Not very strong, just a faint odor, then after it straightens out, no more smell, and it does not display this symptom again until it has sat, say overnight.<BR>  Did have a very slight coolant loss problem, but turned out to be one of the very small rubber lines from the stat housing.  After replacing it, coolant seems to be staying at constant level.  Am not ruling out a coolant or oil problem, the smoke just doesn't lean me in that direction.  Have dealt with bad valve seals and oil rings before.   <BR>     <BR>     Thanks for the input and ideas!  Keep em coming.<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 05-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||JimIndyRCR||05-30-2000||02:48 AM||||Sounds like a slow leaking injector to me. I've had this happen to two different injectors. They stick open just a little so that fuel drips all night. Replaced the injectors and the prob went away. The test was to pull the plugs just after start-up and then found the bad injector.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sage||05-30-2000||03:29 PM||||JIRCR;  Thats what I was thinking it probably is.  Judging from the rest of the work done by the individual that serviced this car, I would not be surprised to find an injector or two that are not properly seated!  Question for you since you have dealt with this before; do you have to remove the intake to gain access to the injectors and rails?  As I said, have not gotten too far into the v6 as of yet. TIA <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||05-30-2000||05:06 PM||||Get a fuel pressure gauge or A/C manifold gauge set. One gauge will fit the fuel rail. Turn the key on wait 10 seconds and shut it off. See how long it holds pressure. It should hold pressure overnight. <P>The problem is the color of the smoke and the time it takes to clear up. It doesn't sound like fuel. <P>But.........<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sage||05-30-2000||05:50 PM||||Terry; I agree about the smoke, but have never stood behind the car and had someone else start it, so don't know what the initial "blast" really looks like.  It doesn't last more than a second or two then it is gone.  My first thought was valve seals too, but none of the plugs had oil on them, however two of them had a fuel smell to them.  I will borrow a fuel preasure gauge and try your recomendation.  Thanks for taking the time to share your knowledge.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000010||triker||05-30-2000||07:10 PM||||You might try some stop leak in the radiator.  I've become a little gun shy about what I say on here, but I don't think it would hurt anything and if it's a leaky head gasket it might slow it down or stop it altogether.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sage||05-30-2000||11:17 PM||||Triker;  Thanks for the suggestion.  If I can determine that it is indeed coolant, I may try that as a temporary fix.  Got 5,000mi. on an iron duke with a cracked head that way!  Then tore it down and got a different head.  Normally don't like to use the stuff, but it has saved my butt more than once.  Used to have an old chevy truck with a hole in the radiator and put alumiseal in it once a year and never did change the radiator!  Only used it for hauling and it didn't see 2,000 mi. in a years time. Thanks again for taking the time to respond.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000012||terryk||05-30-2000||11:55 PM||||You can have bad seals and not see oil on the plugs.<P>How many miles are on this engine? Did it sit for a long time (months) without being run?<P>Could be antifreeze, but the symptons seems unrelated. (Watch, it will be anitfreeze!).<P>Leaking seals (resulting in a puff of smoke on startup) don't tend to make the engine run rough.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phaeton||05-31-2000||02:59 AM||||Low compression can make an engine run a little rough until the rings get oiled up, then they would stay oiled for many an hour.<P>A leaking injector would be rinsing the oil off the rings in the cylinder causing the slight smell of fuel and rough running until the oil got replaced.<P>Unrelated to the smoke, which does sound like valve seals.<P>I would like to be more specific but you haven't mentioned what color the Fiero rims and calipers have been painted.||||reg||12
Z||000014||Sage||05-31-2000||09:26 AM||||Yes the car sat idle for several months.  It has 121,000 mi.  Yes it could stand a rebuild.  It does carry 125lbs. compression in all 6 cylinders.  When My son bought the car, it did not run.  He had to have it towed home.  <BR>     The guy he bought it from said he paid to have a transmission put in and when he got the car back, it would not run!  <BR>     During the initial investigation, did a compression check.  Then found that the distributor was 180* out! Changed the wires and she fired right up. (Don't ask why the distributor was out to change a tranny, I don't have a clue!Have since turned it around.)<BR>     Got it straigtened out to the point that I wanted to deal with the subject of this thread, and thought someone may have dealt with this situation before and have a simple cure!  Wishful thinking huh?  It really does need to have a rebuild to be done absolutely right, but it runs so good after it warms up, just didn't want to take the time,(or spend the money!)to tear it completly down, as I don't need the car, and I do need my garage!  Thanks to everyone for their suggestions, and I will let you know if I figure out for sure what it is.  Everbody have a good one!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 05-31-2000).]||||reg||1
